<body>
    <a href="#main" class="skip-link">Skip to main content</a>
    
    <div class="theme-toggle" id="themeToggle" role="button" aria-label="Toggle theme" tabindex="0">☀️</div>
    
    <header>
        <h1>RAGFlow API Module</h1>
        <p>HTTP/WebSocket Adapter Layer for Multi-Tenant RAG Platform</p>
    </header>
    
    <main id="main">
        <div class="breadcrumbs">
            <a href="/wiki/ragflow/">RAGFlow</a>
            <span>›</span>
            <span>API Module</span>
        </div>
        
        <section id="overview">
            <h2>Overview</h2>
            <p>The RAGFlow API module is the HTTP/WebSocket adapter layer that connects external clients to the RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) platform core. It implements:</p>
            <ul style="margin-left: 1.5rem; margin-top: 1rem;">
                <li><strong>Async Web Server (Quart):</strong> High-concurrency request handling with Python async/await</li>
                <li><strong>Multi-Tenant Isolation:</strong> Tenant ID filtering at query level, enforced across all data access</li>
                <li><strong>Service Layer Pattern:</strong> 15+ domain services (DocumentService, DialogService, TaskService, etc.) encapsulating business logic and state mutations</li>
                <li><strong>Implicit Task Queue:</strong> Redis-backed document processing with background executor coordination</li>
                <li><strong>Hybrid Async+Sync:</strong> Async handlers + synchronous Peewee ORM, optimal for I/O-bound workloads</li>
                <li><strong>Dual Auth Strategy:</strong> JWT tokens + session-based authentication with password hashing (bcrypt)</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        
        <section id="architecture">
            <h2>Architecture Diagram</h2>
            <div class="mermaid-container">
                <div class="mermaid" data-source="api-architecture">
                    graph TD
                    A["Client<br/>(Web/Mobile/API)"] -->|HTTP/WebSocket| B["Quart Server<br/>(Async Handler)"]
                    B -->|Request Validation| C["Auth Middleware<br/>(JWT Decode)"]
                    C -->|Load Tenant| D["Multi-Tenant Context"]
                    D -->|Route to Handler| E["REST Endpoints<br/>(apps/)"]
                    E -->|Business Logic| F["Service Layer<br/>(15+ Services)"]
                    F -->|Tenant Filtering| G["Peewee ORM<br/>(Synchronous)"]
                    G -->|SQL Queries| H["MySQL/PostgreSQL<br/>(Transactional)"]
                    F -->|Queue Task| I["Redis Queue"]
                    I -->|Poll & Process| J["Background Executor<br/>(Task Worker)"]
                    J -->|Update Progress| K["Redis Cache"]
                    B -->|Progress Stream| L["WebSocket Handler"]
                    K -->|Fetch Status| L
                    L -->|Push Update| A
                    J -->|State Mutation| H
                    M["Error Handler<br/>(Custom Exceptions)"] -->|Convert to HTTP| E
                    N["Connection Pool<br/>(10-20 conns)"] -->|Absorb Contention| G
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>
        
        <section id="design-philosophy">
            <h2>Design Philosophy</h2>
            <div class="intro-box">
                <strong>Key Principle:</strong> Optimize for concurrent I/O with a clear separation between request handling (async) and business logic (service layer). Delegate heavy processing (embeddings, parsing) to background workers instead of blocking HTTP handlers.
            </div>
            
            <h3>Why Async Web Server + Sync ORM?</h3>
            <p>The hybrid approach achieves high concurrency for I/O-bound workloads (uploads, API calls to external LLMs) while keeping business logic simple and debuggable:</p>
            <ul style="margin-left: 1.5rem; margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                <li>Quart (async Flask): Handles concurrent requests without thread-per-request overhead</li>
                <li>Peewee (sync ORM): Simple query API, fewer abstractions to debug, connection pool (10-20) absorbs DB contention</li>
                <li>Task Queue (Redis): Document processing returns immediately; progress updates via WebSocket</li>
                <li>Avoids async ORM complexity: Async drivers (asyncpg, motor) add significant operational burden</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3>Multi-Tenancy Enforcement</h3>
            <p>Every domain object carries a <code>tenant_id</code>. Auth layer loads the user's default tenant and passes it through the entire request lifecycle:</p>
            <ul style="margin-left: 1.5rem; margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                <li>Queries are filtered at ORM level, not application tier</li>
                <li>Missing <code>tenant_id</code> check = security vulnerability</li>
                <li>Services are responsible for enforcing tenant isolation (critical responsibility)</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3>Service Layer: More Than CRUD</h3>
            <p>Domain services do not merely wrap database operations. They coordinate state transitions, manage soft deletes, queue tasks, and handle cross-table mutations atomically. Bypassing a service = bypassing multi-tenancy isolation.</p>
        </section>
        
        <section id="submodules">
            <h2>Sub-Modules</h2>
            <div class="card-grid">
                <a href="/wiki/ragflow/docs/api_doc/apps/" class="card">
                    <h3>apps/</h3>
                    <p>Blueprint routing, REST endpoints, request handlers, and auth middleware. Entry point for all HTTP requests and WebSocket connections.</p>
                </a>
                <a href="/wiki/ragflow/docs/api_doc/db/" class="card">
                    <h3>db/</h3>
                    <p>Peewee ORM models (15+ tables), schema definitions, and the 15+ domain services that coordinate state mutations.</p>
                </a>
                <a href="/wiki/ragflow/docs/api_doc/services/" class="card">
                    <h3>services/</h3>
                    <p>Detailed analysis of all 15+ domain services: DocumentService, DialogService, TaskService, KnowledgebaseService, and others.</p>
                </a>
                <a href="/wiki/ragflow/docs/api_doc/joint_services/" class="card">
                    <h3>joint_services/</h3>
                    <p>Cross-domain services coordinating operations across multiple entities (UserAccount, Memory+Message, Tenant+Model).</p>
                </a>
                <a href="/wiki/ragflow/docs/api_doc/common/" class="card">
                    <h3>common/</h3>
                    <p>Shared utilities: custom exceptions, auth decorators, utility functions, and error handling patterns.</p>
                </a>
                <a href="/wiki/ragflow/docs/api_doc/utils/" class="card">
                    <h3>utils/</h3>
                    <p>Miscellaneous helpers for file operations, serialization, logging, and module-level utilities.</p>
                </a>
            </div>
        </section>
        
        <section id="walkthrough">
            <h2>Document Upload Lifecycle (Narrated Walkthrough)</h2>
            <p>Trace a PDF upload from HTTP request through task execution and progress reporting:</p>
            
            <ol class="walkthrough-steps">
                <li>
                    <strong>HTTP POST /api/documents/upload</strong> — Client sends multipart form with PDF file, document name, and knowledge base ID. Request lands in <code>apps/document_api.py</code> handler.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Auth Middleware Validates JWT</strong> — Token decoded in <code>common/auth.py</code>. User's default tenant extracted; tenant ID passed through request context.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Handler Calls DocumentService.create()</strong> — Business logic layer receives request. Validates: tenant isolation, knowledge base ownership, file format, size limits.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>File Saved to Storage</strong> — PDF persisted to disk or cloud storage. File path stored in Document row.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Document Row Inserted</strong> — Peewee ORM inserts row into <code>documents</code> table. Tenant ID enforced at ORM level.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>TaskService.queue()</strong> — DocumentService delegates heavy lifting to TaskService. Creates Task row with status=<code>pending</code>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Redis Queue Entry Created</strong> — Task ID pushed to Redis list. Background executor polls this queue.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Handler Returns 202 Accepted</strong> — HTTP response sent immediately. Document created, processing queued.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Background Executor Polls Redis</strong> — Separate process (not blocking HTTP handler) dequeues task ID.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Parsing & Chunking</strong> — ParsingService extracts text. ChunkService breaks PDF into semantic chunks. Embeddings generated (external LLM call).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Progress Updated in Redis</strong> — Executor pushes progress events: <code>{ "task_id": "...", "progress": 45 }</code> to Redis.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>WebSocket Handler Fetches Status</strong> — Browser WebSocket listener polls <code>/api/tasks/{task_id}/status</code> or listens to server push.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Client Receives Progress</strong> — UI updates progress bar in real-time without blocking HTTP.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Task Completion</strong> — Executor marks task status=<code>completed</code>. Document now searchable.
                </li>
            </ol>
        </section>
        
        <section id="auth-flow">
            <h2>Authentication & Multi-Tenancy</h2>
            
            <h3>JWT Token Flow</h3>
            <p>All requests require a valid JWT token issued by the identity provider:</p>
            <pre><code>Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9...</code></pre>
            
            <p>Auth middleware in <code>common/auth.py</code> decodes the token and extracts:</p>
            <ul style="margin-left: 1.5rem; margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                <li><code>user_id</code> — Unique user identifier</li>
                <li><code>tenant_id</code> — Default tenant for this user</li>
                <li><code>permissions</code> — Role-based access control (admin, editor, viewer)</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3>Tenant Isolation</h3>
            <p>Multi-tenancy is enforced at three levels:</p>
            <ol style="margin-left: 1.5rem; margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                <li><strong>Auth Layer:</strong> User's tenant ID loaded from token</li>
                <li><strong>ORM Layer:</strong> All queries filtered by <code>WHERE tenant_id = ?</code></li>
                <li><strong>Service Layer:</strong> Services validate tenant ownership before state mutations</li>
            </ol>
            
            <h3>Password Hashing</h3>
            <p>Passwords (if used for local auth) are hashed with bcrypt in <code>apps/auth/password.py</code>:</p>
            <pre><code>import bcrypt
hashed = bcrypt.hashpw(password.encode(), bcrypt.gensalt())
bcrypt.checkpw(password.encode(), hashed)  # verify</code></pre>
        </section>
        
        <section id="key-decisions">
            <h2>Architectural Decisions</h2>
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Decision</th>
                        <th>Alternative</th>
                        <th>Rationale</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Async Web Server (Quart)</td>
                        <td>Synchronous Flask</td>
                        <td>Handle 100+ concurrent uploads without thread-per-request overhead. Optimal for I/O-bound workloads.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Synchronous Peewee ORM</td>
                        <td>Async ORM (asyncpg, motor)</td>
                        <td>Simpler to debug, fewer abstractions. Connection pool absorbs DB contention. Avoids async driver complexity.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Service Layer Pattern</td>
                        <td>Direct ORM queries in handlers</td>
                        <td>Isolate business logic, coordinate state transitions atomically, enforce multi-tenancy consistently.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Implicit Task Queue (Redis)</td>
                        <td>Celery / RabbitMQ</td>
                        <td>Simpler operations (no external broker), Redis widely deployed. Tradeoff: manual retry/dead-letter handling.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Tenant ID at Query Level</td>
                        <td>Application-tier filtering</td>
                        <td>Prevent data leaks from missed filters. ORM-level enforcement is harder to bypass accidentally.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Custom Peewee Fields (JSONField, ListField)</td>
                        <td>JSON columns (native DB types)</td>
                        <td>Portability across MySQL/PostgreSQL. Custom serialization logic stays in Python, not SQL.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Deadlock Retry Pattern</td>
                        <td>Fail on first deadlock</td>
                        <td>MySQL InnoDB deadlocks under high concurrency. Exponential backoff retry improves reliability without re-architecting transactions.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>WebSocket for Real-Time Progress</td>
                        <td>Polling / Server-Sent Events</td>
                        <td>Bidirectional communication. Lower latency than polling. More efficient than HTTP long-polling.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </section>
        
        <section id="directory-map">
            <h2>Directory Structure</h2>
            <pre><code>api/
├── apps/                       # Blueprint routing and REST handlers
│   ├── __init__.py            # Blueprint registration
│   ├── auth/                  # JWT decode, password hashing
│   ├── document_api.py        # /api/documents/* endpoints
│   ├── dialog_api.py          # /api/dialogs/* endpoints
│   ├── task_api.py            # /api/tasks/* endpoints (status polling)
│   └── websocket/             # WebSocket handlers for real-time updates
├── db/
│   ├── db_models.py           # Peewee ORM models (15+ tables)
│   ├── services/              # 15+ domain services
│   │   ├── document_service.py
│   │   ├── dialog_service.py
│   │   ├── task_service.py
│   │   ├── llm_service.py
│   │   └── ...
│   └── joint_services/        # Cross-domain services
│       ├── user_account_service.py
│       ├── memory_message_service.py
│       └── tenant_model_service.py
├── common/
│   ├── auth.py               # Auth decorators, JWT decode
│   ├── exceptions.py         # Custom exception classes
│   └── utils.py              # Shared utility functions
└── utils/
    ├── file_ops.py           # File I/O helpers
    ├── serialization.py      # JSON/pickle serialization
    └── logging.py            # Custom logging setup</code></pre>
        </section>
    </main>
    
    <footer>
        <p>RAGFlow API Module Documentation | Generated 2025-05-06 | <a href="/wiki/ragflow/" style="color: var(--link);">Back to RAGFlow Hub</a></p>
    </footer>
    
    <button class="back-to-top" id="backToTop" aria-label="Back to top"></button>
    
    <script>
        const themeKey = 'neutra-ip-theme';
        const html = document.documentElement;
        const themeToggle = document.getElementById('themeToggle');
        const backToTopBtn = document.getElementById('backToTop');
        
        // Initialize theme
        const currentTheme = localStorage.getItem(themeKey) || 'light';
        html.setAttribute('data-theme', currentTheme);
        updateThemeIcon(currentTheme);
        
        // Theme toggle
        themeToggle.addEventListener('click', toggleTheme);
        themeToggle.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
            if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' ') {
                e.preventDefault();
                toggleTheme();
            }
        });
        
        function toggleTheme() {
            const current = localStorage.getItem(themeKey) || 'light';
            const newTheme = current === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'light';
            html.setAttribute('data-theme', newTheme);
            localStorage.setItem(themeKey, newTheme);
            updateThemeIcon(newTheme);
        }
        
        function updateThemeIcon(theme) {
            themeToggle.textContent = theme === 'light' ? '🌙' : '☀️';
        }
        
        // Back to top button
        window.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
            if (window.scrollY > 300) {
                backToTopBtn.classList.add('visible');
            } else {
                backToTopBtn.classList.remove('visible');
            }
        });
        
        backToTopBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
            window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: 'smooth' });
        });
        
        // Mermaid setup
        function renderMermaidDiagrams(theme) {
            if (typeof mermaid === 'undefined') return;
            
            mermaid.initialize({ 
                startOnLoad: false,
                theme: theme === 'dark' ? 'dark' : 'default',
                securityLevel: 'loose'
            });
            
            document.querySelectorAll('.mermaid').forEach(diagram => {
                try {
                    mermaid.contentLoaderAsync.render('mermaid-' + Math.random(), 
                        diagram.textContent, 
                        (html) => {
                            diagram.innerHTML = html;
                        }, 
                        diagram
                    );
                } catch (e) {
                    console.error('Mermaid render error:', e);
                }
            });
        }
        
        // Render diagrams on load
        if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
            document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => renderMermaidDiagrams(currentTheme));
        } else {
            renderMermaidDiagrams(currentTheme);
        }
        
        // Re-render on theme change
        const observer = new MutationObserver((mutations) => {
            mutations.forEach((mutation) => {
                if (mutation.attributeName === 'data-theme') {
                    const newTheme = html.getAttribute('data-theme');
                    renderMermaidDiagrams(newTheme);
                }
            });
        });
        
        observer.observe(html, { attributes: true });
        
        // Add copy buttons to code blocks
        document.querySelectorAll('pre').forEach((pre) => {
            const code = pre.querySelector('code');
            if (code) {
                const btn = document.createElement('button');
                btn.className = 'copy-btn';
                btn.textContent = 'Copy';
                btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
                    navigator.clipboard.writeText(code.textContent).then(() => {
                        const orig = btn.textContent;
                        btn.textContent = 'Copied!';
                        setTimeout(() => { btn.textContent = orig; }, 2000);
                    });
                });
                pre.appendChild(btn);
            }
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
